A||||6||Wannabe||Carrying "Stuff" on the Rear Deck||||1||
Z||000000||Wannabe||08-04-2001||09:57 PM||||My last Fiero had a luggage rack and I had no use for it. Now, on the formula. I may need that space to cary something. Anybody ever do this without a luggage rack? I need to get 4 wheels with r-compound tires to the track and I don't have a trailer yet. The idea was to remobve the passemger seat and carry 2 wheels inside, one in the trunk and the fourth on the rear deck. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||MD_Fiero||08-04-2001||11:30 PM||||why dont u put the 4th were the spare goes?||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||08-04-2001||11:36 PM||||Ah probably because a full size tire wont fit where the temp spare goes. It says right in the manual that if you want a full size spare it goes in the trunk. <P>I dont see a problem with that. But you will have to strap it to something without damaging the car.<P>How are you going to get back if it breaks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||08-04-2001||11:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MD_Fiero:<BR><B>why dont u put the 4th were the spare goes?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>On my 84, not much more than a wheelbarrow tire will fit where the spare is now(in front) I carried a Coleman 48qt cooler on the rear deck once (Had a rack) for about 70 miles. Not a chunk of ice left when I got there. If you carry a tire back there, at least 1 in 4 will be already warmed up when ya get there <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-05-2001||12:27 PM||||The deck lid will take considerable weight w/o damage. I had the transmission for a minivan on mine once... <P>The problem is tiedown. Even if you have a lugage rack option lid, you can't put allot of load on the tie eyes. Depending on the load, you have to get a line or two under the lid itself. If you don't do that, then the load could tear out the tie points on the rack durring hard braking etc.<P>To prtect paint, use a couple thick soft towels or similar material. don't use carpet. Many carpet fibers are abrasive to painted surface.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||08-07-2001||10:36 AM||||Thanks for the info everyone. I'll probably use some flat nylon straps run to the underside of the deck for hold downs, with felt at the edges and under the tire to protect the paint. If the car breaks down at the track....CAA gets a call  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Joe4x4||08-08-2001||02:55 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Dec20^69_1.jpg"> <BR>We did this last year no prob......<P>(( did not lose one bungee)) lol||||reg||1
